1041 #edb/ref 17592186188035 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188328 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "92a52324-627c-5796-bc4f-ddeb5380f129" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188339 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186188680 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188339 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186188687 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188339 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating a Conditional Variance Using Row Totals" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188339 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186188046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188339 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "abfaf356-6b74-5dfd-8070-e3a60dc372c5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188511 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186189167 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188511 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186189168 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188511 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186189169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188511 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186189183 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188511 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188511 #edb/ref 1045 "Conditional Variance for Discrete Random Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188511 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186189169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188511 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188511 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "2c1b60fe-19a1-5c76-8d58-a10c1ae4790c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188687 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186186402 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188687 #edb/ref 1069 "q-146230" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188687 #edb/ref 1071 "| $f$ | $Y = 1$ | $Y = 2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $X = 1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |\n| $X = 2$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n\nSuppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Find $Var\\begin{bmatrix}Y & X = 1\\end{bmatrix}$ given that $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x, y)$ shown in the table above and the corresponding conditional expected value is $μ_{Y \\mid 1} = \\frac{6}{5}$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188687 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e6e8480d-863e-574b-98a7-52fe067fa786" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188687 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189167 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186189183 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189167 #edb/ref 1061 38837 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189167 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2ef086e9-8163-573e-9575-a78051c9420a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189167 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186188327 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189168 #edb/ref 1061 38849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189168 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ac37dad4-4109-5b6c-a2d0-f6c46e058597" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189168 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186188328 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189169 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186189167 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189169 #edb/ref 1061 38850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189169 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "12bb1f79-4af3-56c1-b495-e654c2cf5d1c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189169 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186189401 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189183 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186189168 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189183 #edb/ref 1061 38984 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189183 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7f447a96-6d58-5356-934d-4d4a94738359" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189183 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186188339 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189401 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables, where the conditional expected value of $X$ given $Y=y$ is given by $\\mu_{X \\mid y}$. In other words,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}\\begin{bmatrix}X\\, & \\,Y=y\\end{bmatrix} = \\mu_{X \\mid y}\n$$\n\nThe **conditional variance of $X$ given $Y=y$** is defined as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}\\begin{bmatrix}X\\, & \\,Y=y\\end{bmatrix} = \\textrm{E}\\big\\begin{bmatrix}(X - \\mu_{X \\mid y} \\big)^2 \\, & \\, Y=y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis is similar to the definition of $\\textrm{Var}[X]$ in that it represents the average (squared) distance of the random variable $X$ from the mean. The difference here is that the expected values are now dependent on the outcome of $Y$.\n\nSimilarly to the case of $\\textrm{Var}[X]$, this often isn't the easiest formula to use in practice. However, it can be shown that the definition of conditional variance is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar\\begin{bmatrix}X & Y = y\\end{bmatrix} &= E\\begin{bmatrix}X^{2} & Y = y\\end{bmatrix} - μ_{X\\begin{vmatrix}y}^{2} \\\\ = [∑_(x)x^{2}f_{X\\mid Y}(x \\mid y)] - μ_{X\\end{vmatrix}y}^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f_{X\\mid Y}(x \\, \\mid \\, y)$ is the conditional PMF of $X$ given $Y$. Note that these formulas are analogous to those for $\\textrm{Var}[X]$.\n\nFinally, we have an analogous definition for the conditional variance of $Y$ given $X=x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar\\begin{bmatrix}Y & X = x\\end{bmatrix} &= E\\begin{bmatrix}(Y - μ_{Y \\mid x})^{2} & X = x\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= E\\begin{bmatrix}Y^{2} & X = x\\end{bmatrix} - μ_{Y\\begin{vmatrix}x}^{2} \\\\ = [∑_(y)y^{2}f_{Y\\mid X}(y \\mid x)] - μ_{Y\\end{vmatrix}x}^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189401 #edb/ref 1065 6431 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189401 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ce4fb1c3-94ee-51c3-b10d-ce6ea0c7ff38" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189401 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
